  "_style": "Always address the user with the respectful plural “ẹ” (ẹ, yín, ẹ̀yin: “Ẹ fi pamọ́”, “olólùfẹ́ yín”), never the familiar “o/rẹ”; the only exception is text the user sends to the partner (pings, letters, reactions such as “Mo ń ronú nípa rẹ”), which stays intimate with “rẹ”. The couple together is “ẹ̀yin méjèèjì” (both of you) or “ẹ méjèèjì”, and “wa” when the couple speaks (“Ọgbà wa”, “Igi wa”). The partner is always “olólùfẹ́ yín” (or the name placeholder %@), never ọkọ/ìyàwó, never “tọkọtaya” (husband-and-wife); Yoruba pronouns (ó, òun, wọ́n) are already gender-neutral, so keep them neutral and never add gendered words like ọkùnrin/obìnrin. Write standard Yoruba in Latin script with full tone marks (à á) and underdots (ẹ ọ ṣ) in precomposed or NFC form, never stripped; keep Coupley, Premium, AI, CoupleSync and emoji unchanged. Use curly quotes “ ” outside and ‘ ’ inside, a single-character ellipsis …, no space before ! ? : ; and keep “ · ”, \\n and every placeholder (%@, %lld, %1$@) exactly. Numbers use Western digits with a comma for thousands and a dot for decimals (1,250 · 3.5); dates follow the system format, with written months in Yoruba lowercase (Ṣẹ́rẹ́, Èrèlè… or “oṣù kìíní”) and day before month (“14 Ọ̀wàrà”). Buttons are short verbs without “ẹ jọ̀wọ́” (“Fipamọ́”, “Fi ránṣẹ́”, “Tún gbìyànjú”); polite “ẹ” imperatives are only for longer instructions. Titles, tabs and buttons use sentence case (capital on the first word only, plus proper names). Plural category is only “other”: Yoruba nouns do not inflect, so write “%lld ọjọ́” and add “àwọn” only when no number is present. A partner's name placeholder %@ is never changed or attached to particles; rephrase so it stands alone (“%@ ti fi ránṣẹ́” rather than fusing words to it). Tone: warm, light and kind, natural app Yoruba, never cheesy or word-for-word.",
